---
import ProsePage from '../../templates/ProsePage.astro';
import { CONDUCT_EMAIL } from '../../config/site.ts';
---

<ProsePage
  locale="en"
  routeKey="conduct"
  title="Code of conduct"
  description="The room only works if everyone can be in it. These are the rules, how to report a problem, and what happens when you do."
>
  <p>
    twente.dev exists so that useful regional connections stop depending on already knowing the
    room. That only works if the room is safe and welcoming for everyone — first-timers, students,
    people who came alone, people whose Dutch or English is a work in progress.
  </p>

  <h2>Expected</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Be concrete and generous: share what broke and what you changed, and let others in.</li>
    <li>Welcome newcomers. If someone is standing alone, that is an invitation, not a verdict.</li>
    <li>
      Respect the language mix. Events are Dutch by default and switch to English whenever
      internationals are in the room; never make anyone feel lesser for either.
    </li>
    <li>Respect a "no" — to a photo, to a follow-up, to a sales conversation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Not accepted</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Harassment, intimidation or discrimination in any form, online or in the room.</li>
    <li>Unwelcome sexual attention or physical contact.</li>
    <li>Sustained disruption of talks or conversations.</li>
    <li>
      Recruiting or selling to people who did not ask for it. This is not a recruitment fair; treat
      attendees as peers, not leads.
    </li>
    <li>Photographing or recording someone who has indicated they do not want that.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reporting</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${CONDUCT_EMAIL}`}>{CONDUCT_EMAIL}</a> — it reaches Ryan Grippeling, the organiser,
    who is introduced at the start of every event and recognisable in the room, so you can also simply
    walk up to him. twente.dev is run by one person, so there is no separate committee — that is said
    here plainly rather than dressed up.
  </p>
  <p>
    Is your report about the organiser himself? Then this address is obviously the wrong route:
    raise it with the venue host on the night, or with anyone you trust — and nothing here stops you
    from saying it in public. Reports are handled confidentially, limited only by safety or a legal
    obligation — never by a sponsor's or employer's interest.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens with a report</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>We listen first, and ask what outcome you need — including "just log it".</li>
    <li>
      We act proportionally: a conversation, a warning, removal from the event, or exclusion from
      future events. The person reporting is never required to confront anyone.
    </li>
    <li>
      Incidents are documented and reviewed, so patterns are caught even when single reports seem
      small.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Photography</h2>
  <p>
    Photography at events is consent-based. At check-in you can visibly indicate that you do not
    want to be photographed, and after the event there is a simple route to have a photo removed —
    no reason required.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <p>
    This code applies at every twente.dev event and in every space we run — including this site's
    repository and any future online channels. Organisers, speakers, volunteers, partners and
    sponsors are held to it exactly like everyone else.
  </p>
</ProsePage>
